WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
I'm on the 19th floor of One Canada Square in London

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.100
and that is a GORGEOUS sunset .

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:08.100
And soon the sun will dip below the horizon

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.660
and it will be twilight.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.740
See, night doesn't technically start--

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.620
at least under formal definitions --

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.720
until the sun is 18° below the horizon.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:21.140
And until then, there are three types of twilight:

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.400
First of all there is civil twilight,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.180
that's when the sky is still fairly light and you can walk about without worrying you're going to trip over something,

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.000
that's 0° to 6° below the horizon.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.540
Next up is nautical twilight,

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:38.760
that's when you can just about see the horizon against the sky if you're navigating on a boat,

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.740
that's 6° to 12°.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.620
And finally there is astronomical twilight,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.600
where it's still just a little bit too light

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.280
for the really faint objects like nebulae and galaxies

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.740
to come out in the sky,

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.900
that: 12° to 18°.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.600
And then finally, when the sun tips under that,

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.680
then it formally becomes night.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.640
But the thing is in midsummer,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.800
when it's really really bright,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.880
when it's the sun, high in the sky

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.480
the United Kingdom is north enough,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.560
that it never becomes night,

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.920
that from evening to morning we are perpetually in twilight,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.800
until finally Autumn rolls around and we dip into night once again.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.000
America doesn't quite get it,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.040
Vancouver in Canada just stretches into that definition,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.360
but the whole of the UK,

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.720
and of course a lot of northern Europe,

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:32.100
well, we have entire seasons when we never ever technically have night.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:33.800
That is twilight,

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.360
that is a really gorgeous sunset

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.820
and that is something you might not have known.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.640
[Closed captions by MM
